III. Slated for implementation:


5. Transition interface layer from static Protocols to runtime-enforced ABCs @consultant do not alter preamble or purpose!
    preamble: Shift structural duck-typing to explicit nominal contracts to enforce runtime guarantees without relying on external static analysis tools.
        - Protocols outsource contract verification to external tools (IDEs, linters, mypy) and remain invisible to the Python interpreter at runtime.
        - ABCs leverage Python's native runtime to fail fast at instantiation time whenever concrete classes deviate from their defined contracts.
        - Moving contracts into an isolated module breaks circular dependency cycles, allowing consumers and suppliers to depend downward on shared ABCs safely.
    backlog item purpose: we revert to ABC as interface mechanism
        - now that the codebase separates out the 'interfaces' in files separate to their consumers and separate to their providers
            - no issues of circular dependency is incurred (an issue at the time of switch from abc to protocol, if memory serves) 
    status: only below remains. I decided to step back and chew on it, cuz the collector/report spans several layers of the system.
